        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Secondary noblemen are regular subjects of Maya art, with costumes and proportions that signal their particular social roles. The man wearing a tight vest while wielding a shield is a warrior, while the diminutive proportions of the figure to the right suggest a dwarf, an important court official. While the elegantly proportioned heads carved from shells at lower left likely reference lords, the central figure, who sits on a throne, is a sahal, a type of secondary noble, as confirmed by hieroglyphic captions on other examples. The rightmost figure is a musician with swirling song emanating from his mouth.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA2_20-T6-2_CLA_FA_9_17_25.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
